<Fragment slot="heading">Why "agent" is doing too much work as a <span style="font-style:italic">word</span></Fragment>
<p style="margin:0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Ask ten people in tech what an "agent" is and you'll get ten different answers. The annoying part is that most of them aren't wrong. They're describing different things and reaching for the same word because nothing better was on offer.</p>
<p style="margin:22px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">The big labs are no tidier. <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2025/03/14/no-one-knows-what-the-hell-an-ai-agent-is/" class="src">TechCrunch</a> caught OpenAI using two different definitions in the same week. One post called agents "automated systems that can independently accomplish tasks". Another called them "LLMs equipped with instructions and tools". Those describe two quite different products.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">Where the split actually sits</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Strip the marketing away and there's a real distinction underneath. <a href="https://www.nngroup.com/articles/definition-ai-agent/" class="src">Nielsen Norman Group's version</a> is the cleanest I've found: an agent "pursues a goal by iteratively taking actions, evaluating progress, and deciding its own next steps." A chatbot explains how to do something. An agent has a go at doing it. Anthropic draw much the same line in their <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents" class="src">engineering notes</a>. In a "workflow", code someone else wrote steers the model. In an "agent", the model directs its own process. By that test, most of what gets called an agent today is a workflow with a better job title.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-family:Newsreader,serif;font-size:25px;line-height:1.25;max-width:52ch">"Calling something an agent is a claim about how much you trust it to act without you."</div>
<p style="margin:34px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">The loose label suits people who are selling. Anyone who sits through vendor pitches for a living has the same complaint, and one analyst told <a href="https://www.cio.com/article/3968832/what-makes-a-true-ai-agent-cios-struggle-with-the-definition-as-hype-blurs-lines.html" class="src">CIO.com</a> he has watched "basic automation" get "rebranded as autonomous agents" dozens of times. Once a word stops meaning something specific, it stops helping you decide what to buy or how to govern it. It also can't tell your stakeholders what the thing will do on the day nobody is watching.</p>
<div style="margin-top:26px;border:1px dashed var(--dash)"><div data-shot="1" style="height:190px;background:var(--stripe);display:flex;align-items:flex-end;padding:20px"><span style="font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:10.5px;color:var(--muted);letter-spacing:.08em">[ OPTIONAL DIAGRAM ]</span></div></div>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">The plain version</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">So when someone tells you they've built an agent, ask them two things. What can it do without checking with a person? And what picks the next step, the model or code somebody wrote? If the answers are "not much" and "the code", you're looking at a workflow. That's fine, and often safer, but it should be priced, tested and governed as a workflow.</p>
